※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1
※ pl4g-test: run=native,qemu exit=7
※ pl4g-test: diag=none

※ A function answering with more values than the convention carries in
※ registers is rewritten to write its answer into a place the caller provides.
※ That makes it write memory that outlives the call, which is not the same
※ thing as making it impure: what it writes is the place it was handed and
※ nothing else.  So a caller that reads none of that place may still drop the
※ call, and the room it made goes with it.
※
※ Here the arm that reads the answer is the one the condition never takes, and
※ the condition is settled once `pick` stands where it was called.  What the
※ program answers with is the other arm, and it answers with it whether or not
※ the call was made -- which is what says dropping it was allowed.

@[inline(never)]
fn three() → 〈u8, u8, u8〉:
    〈1u8, 2u8, 3u8〉

fn pick(c: bool) → u8:
    let t: 〈u8, u8, u8〉 = three()
    if c:
        t⟦0⟧
    else:
        7u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(pick(false), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
